WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.360
I just cannot stand thinking about

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:09.480
something and not acting on it. Life is

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.400
so short. Time passes you by so fast.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.680
All the time spent talking about it, you

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.560
could be 6 months ahead.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.320
I'm Sam Parr. I'm a serial entrepreneur

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.000
and my companies make over $10 million a

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.920
year. A lot of people ask me how I spend

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.680
my time or how I decide what ideas I'm

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.760
going to work on. So, today I'm excited

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.760
to tell you about how I work.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.600
I get up between 6:00 and 6:30 in the

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.960
morning. I immediately go and get some

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.720
black coffee for my Nespresso and I put

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.160
a little bit of whole milk in there and

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.240
then I try to be in the gym just 20 or

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.960
30 minutes after that. I lift weights

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.000
and I lift heavy or I'm outside on a

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.840
run. And my run is mostly looks like

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.840
sprinting, but I always try to get a

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.440
workout in or some type of exercise in

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.600
on the morning. I exercise roughly 6

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.400
days a week. If I'm injured or on rest

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.720
days, I am always stretching. I stretch

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.640
like a madman cuz I love to stretch and

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.400
no matter what, without fail, I have

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.520
breakfast with my kids.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.280
Obviously, I'm addicted to my phone. So,

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.480
I don't sleep with my phone in my

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.320
bedroom. I actually don't look at my

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.240
phone until right before my workout and

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.800
when I look at my phone, it's because A,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.240
I have to load my workout and B, I'm

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.280
about to brick my phone. Originally, I

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.040
used a timed KitchenAid container and

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.640
one time I needed to access my phone so

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.800
badly, I smashed the container. I tested

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.520
an Opal, which is like an app, but now I

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.120
really love Brick. So, I've been using

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.120
Brick now for over a year.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.120
The problem with like social media,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.440
particularly with Twitter, is that

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.120
everyone seems like they're killing it.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.480
Well, the truth is is I've seen behind

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.040
the scenes of almost all of these

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.479
businesses and all of these types of

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.960
people. I have talked to thousands of

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.080
entrepreneurs. At conferences, I've

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.440
talked to billionaires. And I can tell

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.480
you everyone has a lot of complaints and

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.800
everyone at times wishes they were doing

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.680
something else. Being analog and not

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.600
being distracted, it's literally just a

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.280
matter of like not comparing yourself to

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.760
other people, which is very toxic and a

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.000
trait that I tend to do. So, I need to

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.080
like turn that off so I just focus on my

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.920
one thing. And nine out of 10 times,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.320
that seems to be a better solution than

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.320
bailing and starting something new.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.120
When getting ready for work, I do

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.400
journal. I have this thing called a

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.600
five-year journal and I write how I feel

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.120
that day and I could see how I felt that

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.000
same day the year before and the year

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.720
before. So, there's been multiple times

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.720
where I've seen I'm going to complain

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.040
about something or say that I'm

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.320
frustrated about something that day in

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.800
that journal and I complained about the

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.520
same day the year before. And I consider

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.560
myself an entrepreneur who makes actions

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.440
and makes things happen, but unless I

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.519
clearly set up a system, even in my own

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:04.160
personal life, nothing will change. So,

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:05.680
if I complain that I don't feel as fit

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.480
as I want to be, but I didn't hire a

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.040
coach, then I'm not going to change.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.160
So, I live and I work in a very

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.040
purposeful way that my commute usually

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.480
only takes about 13 minutes. What's

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.519
funny is I own motorcycles, I love cars,

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.360
I love all of this stuff, but half the

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:25.640
time I ride a little rinky-dink scooter

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.280
to work. The other half, if it's raining

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.519
or I just want to like get more steps

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:33.080
in, I take the subway. And I love to

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:35.280
dress nice for work. Make fun of me all

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.200
you want, but I love like picking out my

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.080
outfits the day before or thinking about

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.680
what I'm going to wear because I like to

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.680
look cool.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.560
I am a lazy person. I procrastinate a

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.600
ton. And so, the only times that I get

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.960
things done is when they're time-bound

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.800
and they're specific. In the morning, I

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.960
have active planning where I write down

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.520
what I need to get done. So, it's like I

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:05.600
need to come up with like three ideas

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:08.000
for a podcast. I write it down and then

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.120
I go to my calendar and I time-bound it.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.760
And my goal, by the way, I've learned

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:15.640
this over the years is I can only do

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.640
probably one or two things a day.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:24.240
My love for podcasting, every once in a

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.360
while it will ebb and flow.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.320
The times that I will dislike it for

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.880
weeks or months at a time is when I find

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.600
myself falling into the trap of chasing

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.320
numbers. I'll start with like ideas that

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.480
I know will get views or downloads, but

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.200
don't like make me happy. But then other

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.120
times when I get really happy, like I'm

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.120
super happy about the podcast right now,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.040
it's when I talk to people and they

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.000
break and it breaks my frame. I'll talk

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.520
to someone who has raised money in this

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.600
field or someone who has bootstrapped in

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.400
this other field. And the number one

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.080
takeaway of talking to the people who I

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.440
talk to on the podcast and in my life is

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.160
that there's so many different paths to

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.400
get to where you want to go. And that

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.840
has given me so much confidence. I give

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.840
so much confidence in that.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.320
So, I podcast every Monday and

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.960
Wednesday. I have a Claude skill that

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.919
looks at my calendar and it will see

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.080
who's the guest that day and it will

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:20.919
automatically do research on them. And

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.240
I'll read that research. And then I will

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:25.919
go and read books on the topic or I will

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.840
go and research a bunch of stuff about

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:30.000
that person. 10 minutes before I have to

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.720
record, so about 11:50, I hurry up and I

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.160
go pee so I don't have to go to the

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.400
bathroom when I'm talking to them. And

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.280
then I come and I sit down and I record.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.520
After I record, I just need to like

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.840
decompress. And the way that I

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.760
decompress is I just like meander and

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.520
wander. So, I usually will just walk

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.880
around my office and I'll say, "What's

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.440
up? What are you doing?" And they'll

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.040
tell me and I'll say, "You seem

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.520
unhappy." Or, "You seem like you have a

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.680
problem. What's the problem?" And

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.520
they'll tell me. And I have to like,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.440
"Okay, that does sound like a problem.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.880
Let's go and solve it." And we had to

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.160
put frosties, like frosted glass on the

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.600
windows, because everyone's like, "Dude,

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.440
it's creepy how you just walk around and

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.680
look in the windows." I'm not sure if

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.680
it's right or wrong to do that, but I'm

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:12.040
obsessed. I can't go to sleep at night

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.040
knowing something is wrong.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.480
Typically, people who are high achievers

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:23.840
have a huge sense of urgency. There's

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.720
this amazing study, also. They took two

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.520
classes of college kids. One class, they

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.919
said to the kids, "The goal of the class

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.880
is to make as many pots as you possibly

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:35.080
can." The other class, they said, "Your

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.960
goal is to make the most perfect pot."

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.480
The group of people who made huge

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:41.200
amounts of quantity were significantly

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.640
happier with the outcome, because they

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.360
were able to perfect it as they went

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.840
along, and they got so much better. The

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.160
class that went for that one, their pots

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.080
were less good, and they were way more

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.440
unhappy with the outcome. If you're just

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.280
trying to get your first million, your

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.400
first few million, ceaseless action is

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.400
the way that you get it.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:07.480
From 0 to 5 million, or maybe 0 to 10

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.160
million in revenue, for every company

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:13.440
that I've run, I am blunt force, where I

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.760
just will have what I call zebra

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.360
calendar, because there's a stripe,

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.280
which is like a 15-minute meeting with a

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.120
customer, and then 5-minute break, and

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.360
then another one, and then another one.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.160
And I'm just brute forcing this thing to

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.919
like make it work. I'll make the

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:30.480
website. I'll write all the copy. I'll

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:32.960
do all the design work. From like 0 to

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.680
10 million, to me, it's a matter of

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.200
getting more customers and making the

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:39.560
product better. And I don't really spend

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.480
too much time thinking about what to do.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:44.120
I just say, "What's next? Go. What's

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.840
next? Go. What's next? Go." But, the

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.400
truth is is that once a company gets to

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.560
like 5 or 10 million in revenue, you

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.640
should delegate and you should hire. And

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.520
once I do that, I'm like sitting there,

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.280
I'm like, "I don't know what to do with

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.880
my hands. Like, what do I do?" And I

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.240
have little toy or a play box sandbox

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.040
that I can like mess with stuff so I

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.520
don't mess with the main thing. When you

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.520
have something that's compounding and

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.160
working, you let it go and you don't

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.160
mess with it.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.720
What people don't realize is you have to

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.240
figure out who you are. Are you a person

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:19.480
who needs encouragement to get started?

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.760
If you are, act. Just start on whatever

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:22.640
and get a little bit of results and then

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.919
pivot and maybe a little bit more. But,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.320
if you have a track record where you're

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:26.919
like, "I'm already proven that I'm a

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.320
little bit of a terminator. I achieve

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:31.240
goals." Then, spending time plotting and

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:33.159
picking the right project that fits

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.400
their what I call it ikigai, which is

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:36.159
what the world wants, what the world

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.640
wants to pay for, what you're good at,

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:39.159
and what you love doing. Picking

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.440
something right in the middle of that,

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:44.680
that is key if you have a proven track

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.680
record of doing stuff.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.800
For the first year of the hustle, when I

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.360
told successful people what I was doing,

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.160
they were like, "Okay, but what are you

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.240
actually going to do?" Or when I told my

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.480
friends what I was doing, they were

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.360
like, "So, how do you pay the bills? Or

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.560
like, do you have an actual job?" Like,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.880
they thought it was stupid. Now, a

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.640
newsletter or a podcast is like, "Oh,

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.839
that's what you do. I understand." I've

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.240
seen like things like that happen over

00:09:12.839 --> 00:09:17.880
and over and over again to where I just

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.440
know that like cool stuff seems dumb at

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.440
first.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:30.080
My evenings are almost embarrassing. I

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.960
get home between 5:30 and 6:00. We

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:35.480
either order sushi or cook at home. I

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.640
have dinner with my kids. I love it. We

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:40.040
listen to music sometimes while we have

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:42.880
dinner and it's so much fun and I put my

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.600
little girl down between 8:00 or 8:30

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:48.480
and that's my favorite time of day. From

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:51.440
around 8:30 or 9:00 to about 10:00, I

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.480
like to search stuff on eBay. I spend so

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.720
much time on eBay. I just love finding

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.200
cool stuff and cool treasures. It's also

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:01.000
Claude time. While the eBay search is

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.760
loading, I'm on Claude asking it

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:05.120
questions about life. It's like therapy

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:08.520
time. And then I lay in bed at 10:00 and

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.520
from 10:00 to about 10:45 I'm reading. I

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:13.280
love going on what I call semesters. So

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.640
I'll read like five to 10 books on the

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.880
same topic. Right now, I'm in a serial

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.760
killer semester, baby. Before that, it

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.960
was shipwrecks. Before that, it was the

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:25.000
Kennedy family. Before that, it was like

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:25.000
New York history.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:30.520
A lot of times at like 10:30, I'll sneak

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.760
out to the kitchen and I'll have like an

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:37.760
Oreo. I do that all the time. Calories

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.760
don't count if it's after 10:00 p.m.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.360
I don't think people understand how

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:48.880
enthusiasm and the right mindset can

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:50.680
sort of crush any obstacle. It's if I

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:53.360
think I can or if I think I can't, I'm

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:54.880
probably right. Like if I'm enthusiastic

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.920
enough, I'm going to make my energy

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:58.920
contagious and the people around me are

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:00.400
going to be enthused and excited and

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.920
they're the one who are going to do all

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.680
of the work and actually make the dream

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.440
a reality. And so I don't think you

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.800
should not do something because it hurts

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.240
or it sucks. I love working out. It's

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.800
really hard. I love building companies.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:15.920
It's really hard. It's kind of cheesy,

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:19.800
but it's like you have to feel pain in

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:19.800
order to like discover greatness.

